pub-id-type="pmid">1068466</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1073/pnas.73.10.3549</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B41"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Berman</surname><given-names>HM</given-names></name><name><surname>Westbrook</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Feng</surname><given-names>Z</given-names></name><name><surname>Gilliland</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Bhat</surname><given-names>TN</given-names></name><name><surname>Weissig</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Shindyalov</surname><given-names>IN</given-names></name><name><surname>Bourne</surname><given-names>PE</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The Protein Data Bank</article-title><source>Nucleic Acids Res</source><year>2000</year><volume>28</volume><fpage>235</fpage><lpage>242</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10592235</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1093/nar/28.1.235</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B42"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Atilgan</surname><given-names>AR</given-names></name><name><surname>Durell</surname><given-names>SR</given-names></name><name><surname>Jernigan</surname><given-names>RL</given-names></name><name><surname>Demirel</surname><given-names>MC</given-names></name><name><surname>Keskin</surname><given-names>O</given-names></name><name><surname>Bahar</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Anisotropy of fluctuation dynamics of proteins with an elastic network model</article-title><source>Biophys J</source><year>2001</year><volume>80</volume><fpage>505</fpage><lpage>515</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11159421</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B43"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bahar</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name><name><surname>Wallqvist</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Covell</surname><given-names>DG</given-names></name><name><surname>Jernigan</surname><given-names>RL</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Correlation between native-state hydrogen exchange and cooperative residue fluctuations from a simple model</article-title><source>Biochemistry</source><year>1998</year><volume>37</volume><fpage>1067</fpage><lpage>1075</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9454598</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1021/bi9720641</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B44"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Haliloglu</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Bahar</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Structure-based analysis of protein dynamics: comparison of theoretical results for hen lysozyme with X-ray diffraction and NMR relaxation data</article-title><source>Proteins</source><year>1999</year><volume>37</volume><fpage>654</fpage><lpage>667</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10651280</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1002/(SICI)1097-0134(19991201)37:4&#x0003c;654::AID-PROT15&#x0003e;3.0.CO;2-J</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B45"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Demirel</surname><given-names>MC</given-names></name><name><surname>Atilgan</surname><given-names>AR</given-names></name><name><surname>Jernigan</surname><given-names>RL</given-names></name><name><surname>Erman</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Bahar</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Identification of kinetically hot residues in proteins</article-title><source>Protein Sci</source><year>1998</year><volume>7</volume><fpage>2522</fpage><lpage>2532</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9865946</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><sec sec-type="display-objects"><title>Figures and Tables</title><fig position="float" id="F1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>A schematic representation of multispecific ligand recognition by antibodies. (A) Rigid adaptation, (B) Induced fit model, (C) Differential ligand positioning with preexisting equilibrium model</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1472-6807-7-31-1"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Temperature factors of (A) unbound-LAO (PDB <ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="2lao">2lao</ext-link>) and (B) unbound antibody- heavy chain (PDB <ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="2a6j">2a6j</ext-link>). The dashed lines represent the results of our calculations; solid lines are the experimental temperature factors. Theoretical temperature factors are obtained by using all the collective modes of the proteins. The residues 137&#x02013;140 are not shown for <ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="2a6j">2a6j</ext-link>.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1472-6807-7-31-2"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F3"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>The pathway followed by the for LAO-binding Protein. The left most and right most structures are the unbound and bound conformations, respectively. The intermediate structures are obtained by adding the second global eigenvector to the original coordinates. Since the eigenvectors are normalized to one, a scaling factor of 40, 50 and 60 are used to obtain the intermediate structures. The rmsd values of the intermediate structures are 2.68 (2.55), 2.33 (3.18) and 2.13 (3.82) from the bound (unbound) structure. The two domains of the proteins close as the ligand is bound to the protein.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1472-6807-7-31-3"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F4"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Comparison of theoretical root mean square fluctuations of (A) unbound-LAO (PDB <ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="2lao">2lao</ext-link>) and (B) 36&#x02013;65 Fab, antibody- heavy chain (PDB <ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="2a6j">2a6j</ext-link>) with the experimentally known conformational changes (&#x00394;R = R<sub>o </sub>- R<sub>c</sub>). The solid curves represent the experimental changes whereas the dashed curves are obtained from the theory. Note that the theoretical curve is scaled to have the same area under the curve with the experimental one. The theoretical fluctuations that are driven by the second and third global modes are shown for LAO and the antibody fragment, respectively.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1472-6807-7-31-4"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F5"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>The ribbon diagrams of (A) the unbound 36&#x02013;65 Fab antibody and (B-D) the 36&#x02013;65 Fab antibody with three different peptides bound: Rll, RLLIDPPSPRE, KIa, KLASIPTHTSPL, and Slg, SLGDNLTNHNLR, respectively. The peptide atoms are displayed as black spheres. The PDB codes of the unbound antibody is <ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="2a6j">2a6j</ext-link>. The three PDB codes of the complexes with three different dodecamers are <ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="2a6d">2a6d</ext-link>, <ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="2a6i">2a6i</ext-link> and <ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="2a6k">2a6k</ext-link>.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1472-6807-7-31-5"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F6"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p>The detailed binding sites of (A) the unbound 36&#x02013;65 Fab antibody and (B-D) the 36&#x02013;65 Fab antibody with three different peptides bound: Rll, RLLIDPPSPRE, KIa, KLASIPTHTSPL, and Slg, SLGDNLTNHNLR, respectively.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1472-6807-7-31-6"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F7"><label>Figure 7</label><caption><p>A schematic representation of the similarities of four different conformers of the SPE7 antibody. Part (A) shows the rmsds among the different conformers. Part (B) indicates the most relevant mode to drive the unbound antibodies to the bound ones. The numbers in the parentheses show the responsible mode numbers.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1472-6807-7-31-7"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F8"><label>Figure 8</label><caption><p>Comparison of global modes with the real displacements for the SPE7 antibody. Dashed lines are for the experimental displacements. Solid lines represent the residue fluctuations for the first and second global modes, respectively (Parts <bold>A </bold>and <bold>B</bold>). The insets are the crystal structures of SPE7. The top structures are for the unbound conformers (<ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="1oaq">1oaq</ext-link> and <ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="1ocw">1ocw</ext-link>). The lower panel shows the bound conformations (<ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="1oau">1oau</ext-link> and <ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="1oaz">1oaz</ext-link>). The purple highlighted regions are the antigen binding regions.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1472-6807-7-31-8"/></fig><table-wrap position="float" id="T1"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>List of proteins studied.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><td align="left">Protein ID (unbound-bound)</td><td align="left">Name of the protein</td><td align="left">Number of residues</td><td align="left">Ligand</td><td align="left">Rmsd between the structures (&#x000c5;)</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left"><ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="3chy">3chy</ext-link>-<ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="1chn">1chn</ext-link></td><td align="left">CheY</td><td align="left">128</td><td align="left">Mg</td><td align="left">1.39</td></tr><tr><td align="left"><ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="5dfr">5dfr</ext-link>-<ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="4dfr">4dfr</ext-link>^</td><td align="left">Dihydrofolate Reductase</td><td align="left">158</td><td align="left">Methotrexate</td><td align="left">0.80</td></tr><tr><td align="left"><ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="1ctr">1ctr</ext-link>-<ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="1cll">1cll</ext-link></td><td align="left">Calmodulin</td><td align="left">138</td><td align="left">Trifluoperazine</td><td align="left">14.4</td></tr><tr><td align="left"><ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="2lao">2lao</ext-link>-<ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="1lst">1lst</ext-link></td><td align="left">K-, R-, Orn-Binding Protein</td><td align="left">238</td><td align="left">Lys</td><td align="left">4.70</td></tr><tr><td align="left"><ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="3tms">3tms</ext-link>-<ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="2tsc">2tsc</ext-link></td><td align="left">Thymidylate Synthase</td><td align="left">264</td><td align="left">UMP, CB3</td><td align="left">0.80</td></tr><tr><td align="left"><ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="1hka">1hka</ext-link>-<ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="1rao">1rao</ext-link></td><td align="left">HPPK (kinase)</td><td align="left">158</td><td align="left">AMP</td><td align="left">3.13</td></tr><tr><td align="left"><ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="1bbs">1bbs</ext-link>-<ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="1rne">1rne</ext-link></td><td align="left">Renin</td><td align="left">326</td><td align="left">CGP, NAG</td><td align="left">6.16</td></tr><tr><td align="left"><ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="2a">2a</ext-link><ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="6j">6j</ext-link>-<ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="2a6d">2a6d</ext-link>*</td><td align="left">Antibody-Heavy chain</td><td align="left">222</td><td align="left">Dodecapeptide1</td><td align="left">0.94</td></tr><tr><td align="left"><ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="2a6j">2a6j</ext-link>-<ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="2a6i">2a6i</ext-link>*</td><td align="left">Antibody-Heavy chain</td><td align="left">222</td><td align="left">Dodecapeptide2</td><td align="left">1.10</td></tr><tr><td align="left"><ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="2a6j">2a6j</ext-link>-<ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="2a6k">2a6k</ext-link>*</td><td align="left">Antibody-Heavy chain</td><td align="left">222</td><td align="left">Dodecapeptide3</td><td align="left">1.00</td></tr><tr><td align="left"><ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="1oaq">1oaq</ext-link>-<ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="1oau">1oau</ext-link></td><td align="left">Antibody-Heavy chain</td><td align="left">121</td><td align="left">DNP-Ser</td><td align="left">0.62</td></tr><tr><td align="left"><ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="1ocw">1ocw</ext-link>-<ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="1oaz">1oaz</ext-link></td><td align="left">Antibody-Heavy chain</td><td align="left">121</td><td align="left">Trx-Shear3</td><td align="left">1.91</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><p>The first column lists the unbound and bound conformations PDB codes. The second column gives the protein description; the third column gives the residue numbers of the proteins. The fourth column lists the ligand bound to the protein. The last column provides the root mean square deviation (rmsd) between the unbound &#x02013; bound structures considering the alpha carbons.</p><p>^The alignments are calculated over two stretches: 1&#x02013;15 and 21&#x02013;159.</p><p>* The alignments are calculated over two stretches: 1&#x02013;136 and 141&#x02013;222.</p></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap position="float" id="T2"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Correlation coefficients between the real displacements and theoretical values</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><td align="left">Mode #</td><td align="left"><ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="3chy">3chy</ext-link></td><td align="left"><ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="5dfr">5dfr</ext-link></td><td align="left"><ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="1ctr">1ctr</ext-link></td><td align="left"><ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="2lao">2lao</ext-link></td><td align="left"><ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="3tms">3tms</ext-link></td><td align="left"><ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="1hka">1hka</ext-link></td><td align="left"><ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="1bbs">1bbs</ext-link></td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left">1</td><td align="left">0.60</td><td align="left"><bold>0.66</bold></td><td align="left">0.47</td><td align="left">0.37</td><td align="left">0.10</td><td align="left">0.72</td><td align="left">0.60</td></tr><tr><td align="left">2</td><td align="left"><bold>0.73</bold></td><td align="left">0.42</td><td align="left">0.14</td><td align="left"><bold>0.84</bold></td><td align="left"><bold>0.70</bold></td><td align="left">0.85</td><td align="left">0.40</td></tr><tr><td align="left">3</td><td align="left">0.68</td><td align="left">0.43</td><td align="left"><bold>0.63</bold></td><td align="left">0.23</td><td align="left">0.05</td><td align="left"><bold>0.87</bold></td><td align="left"><bold>0.71 </bold>(4)</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><p>The first column gives the mode numbers, the first row lists the PDB IDs of the unbpund structures. The bold numbers highlights the highest correlation coefficient for the respective protein.</p></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap position="float" id="T3"><label>Table 3</label><caption><p>Correlation coefficients between the real displacements and theoretical values for the antibody bound to three different dodecamers.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><td align="left">PDB ID of the Unbound-Bound Antibody</td><td align="left">Correlation coefficient</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left"><ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="2a6j">2a6j</ext-link>-<ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="2a6d">2a6d</ext-link></td><td align="left">0.66 (3)</td></tr><tr><td align="left"><ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="2a6j">2a6j</ext-link>-<ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="2a6i">2a6i</ext-link></td><td align="left">0.69 (3)</td></tr><tr><td align="left"><ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="2a6j">2a6j</ext-link>-<ext-link ext-link-type="pdb" xlink:href="2a6k">2a6k</ext-link></td><td align="left">0.71 (3)</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><p>The first column lists the PDB IDs of the unbound and bound structures of the antibodies. The number in the parentheses correspond to the mode numbers.</p></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></sec></back></article>